import{_ as o,o as t,c,a2 as a}from"./chunks/framework.CFryVJGt.js";const g=JSON.parse('{"title":"Industrial-grade gap analysis","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"notes/industrial-grade-gap-analysis.md","filePath":"notes/industrial-grade-gap-analysis.md"}'),s={name:"notes/industrial-grade-gap-analysis.md"};function d(n,e,i,r,l,p){return t(),c("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[a('<h1 id="industrial-grade-gap-analysis" tabindex="-1">Industrial-grade gap analysis <a class="header-anchor" href="#industrial-grade-gap-analysis" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Industrial-grade gap analysis&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Fact-finding against primary sources only (source, config, CI, git history — no external best-practice references taken on faith). Scope: what&#39;s missing for this library to be &quot;industry best / industrial grade&quot; — stability, code quality, release hygiene. All citations are <code>file:line</code> or command output from this repo (<code>develop</code> branch, HEAD <code>a21089b</code>) unless noted. Ends with a prioritized P0/P1/P2 punch list as requested.</p><p>Out of scope by request: whether CI should move from Jenkins to GitHub Actions, and open-source contributor hygiene (<code>CONTRIBUTING.md</code>, <code>CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md</code>, issue/PR templates) — this is an internally-consumed library, not a community-driven OSS project, so neither is a relevant gap here.</p><h2 id="_1-test-coverage-quality" tabindex="-1">1. Test coverage &amp; quality <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-test-coverage-quality" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;1. Test coverage &amp; quality&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Exists:</strong></p><ul><li><code>packages/core</code> has 26 unit test files, 389 passing tests, 1 skipped (<code>npx vitest run -c test/unit/vitest.config.mts</code>, confirmed by running it — all green, 3.95s). Files: <code>packages/core/test/unit/*.test.ts</code> (e.g. <code>links.test.ts</code>, <code>updateStyle.test.ts</code>, <code>detect.test.ts</code>, <code>spatialIndex.test.ts</code>).</li><li><code>packages/core</code> also has a dedicated e2e suite, <code>packages/core/test/e2e/*.test.ts</code> (10 files: <code>creation</code>, <code>comment</code>, <code>stickyNote</code>, <code>multiSelectDrag</code>, <code>anchorFollow</code>, <code>offsetContainer</code>, <code>lockedAndHidden</code>, <code>snapping</code>, <code>drawing</code>, <code>smallFontCulling</code>), driven by Playwright/Chromium via a custom vitest config (<code>packages/core/test/e2e/vitest.config.mts:1-33</code>, <code>retry: 2</code>, <code>maxWorkers: 2</code>, <code>hookTimeout: 60000</code> — config comments describe real CI flakiness tuning, e.g. &quot;a test that takes ~6s locally took 47s in CI&quot;).</li><li>A benchmark script exists: <code>packages/core/package.json:64</code> — <code>&quot;test:benchmark&quot;: &quot;vitest bench --run&quot;</code>.</li><li>Root workspace also runs a small dependency-floor test: <code>test/index.test.ts</code> via <code>test/vitest.config.mts</code> and <code>scripts/test-ogma-floor.mjs</code> (<code>package.json:9-13</code>), which checks behavior against the declared minimum Ogma peer version.</li><li>JUnit XML reporting is wired for CI consumption: <code>packages/core/test/unit/vitest.config.mts:9-10</code> (<code>reporters: [&quot;default&quot;,&quot;junit&quot;]</code>, <code>outputFile: ../../reports/unit/annotations/junit-test-results.xml</code>).</li></ul><p><strong>Missing / weak:</strong></p><ul><li><s><code>packages/react</code> UI layer (16 <code>ui/**</code> components) had zero test coverage</s> — <strong>closed 2026-09-22.</strong> Every component under <code>packages/react/src/ui/**</code> (<code>AddMenu</code>, <code>AnnotationPanel</code>, <code>AnnotationPanelController</code>, <code>TextAnnotationToolbarController</code>, <code>ViewControls</code>, <code>Icon</code>, <code>controllers/{Background,Color,Extremity,Font,LineType,Slider}Controller</code>) now has a co-located <code>*.test.tsx</code> (12 new files, 68 new tests; combined with the pre-existing <code>AnnotationsContext.test.tsx</code>, <code>packages/react</code> is now 13 test files / 80 tests, all passing via <code>npx vitest run</code> in <code>packages/react</code>). Pattern: <code>useAnnotationsContext</code> (self-imported from <code>@linkurious/ogma-annotations-react</code>) and, where relevant, <code>useOgma</code> are <code>vi.mock</code>&#39;d per-test; pure UI data/helpers from <code>@linkurious/ogma-annotations/ui</code> (<code>LINE_TYPES</code>, <code>BACKGROUNDS</code>, <code>FONTS</code>, etc.) are imported for real. Still missing: no coverage numbers exist to quantify how deep this goes (see the coverage-tooling gap below), and the underlying <code>attachPanelVisibility</code> state machine (<code>packages/core/src/ui/panelVisibility.ts</code>) that <code>AnnotationPanelController</code> wires up has no test of its own in <code>packages/core</code> — the React test mocks it rather than exercising it.</li><li>No <code>packages/react</code> e2e suite exists at all (no <code>test/e2e</code> directory under <code>packages/react</code>, confirmed via <code>find</code>).</li><li><strong>No coverage tooling is actually installed or enforced.</strong><code>packages/react/vite.config.ts:51-60</code> configures a <code>test.coverage</code> block (<code>reporter: [&quot;json&quot;,&quot;cobertura&quot;]</code>, <code>reportsDirectory: &quot;reports/coverage&quot;</code>), but <code>@vitest/coverage-v8</code> and <code>@vitest/coverage-istanbul</code> are <strong>not</strong> devDependencies of any package (absent from <code>packages/core/package.json</code>, <code>packages/react/package.json</code>, and root <code>package.json</code>) — they only appear in <code>package-lock.json</code> as optional peerDependencies of <code>vitest</code> itself (<code>package-lock.json:11185-11213</code>), and are not present under <code>node_modules/@vitest/</code> (<code>ls node_modules/@vitest/</code> shows only <code>mocker/</code> and <code>spy/</code>). Running coverage today would fail with a missing-provider error. No coverage numbers exist, no threshold is configured anywhere in the repo (<code>grep -rn &quot;thresholds&quot;</code> across all config/JSON/YAML files: zero hits), and no <code>test:coverage</code> script exists in any <code>package.json</code>.</li><li>CI enforcement of tests is opaque from this repo alone: <code>Jenkinsfile:8</code> sets <code>runUnitTests = true</code> explicitly, but there is no equivalent explicit flag for e2e or lint (<code>runForwardMerge</code>, <code>runDependencyVersionCheck</code>, <code>runNpmPublish</code>, <code>createGitTag</code>, <code>runBookeeping</code> are the only other flags set — see <code>Jenkinsfile:6-16</code>). Whether <code>test:e2e</code> or <code>lint:ci</code> actually run per-PR depends entirely on the external <code>linkurious-shared</code> Jenkins library (<code>Jenkinsfile:1</code>), which is not part of this repo and can&#39;t be verified from primary sources here.</li><li><code>reports/e2e-screenshots/</code> contains 48 <code>*_failed.png</code> files with names matching current e2e test titles (e.g. <code>stickyNote_renders_toggles_...</code>, <code>creation_should_create_one_of_every_annotation_type_...</code>) — evidence that the e2e suite has failed/flaked in past local or CI runs, though this is a stale artifact directory, not proof of current failures (the unit suite run above was clean).</li></ul><h2 id="_2-type-safety" tabindex="-1">2. Type safety <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-type-safety" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;2. Type safety&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Exists:</strong></p><ul><li><code>strict: true</code> in both packages: <code>packages/core/tsconfig.json:14</code>, <code>packages/react/tsconfig.json:26</code>. Both also set <code>noUnusedLocals</code>/<code>noUnusedParameters</code>/<code>noImplicitReturns</code> (<code>packages/core/tsconfig.json:15-17</code>, <code>packages/react/tsconfig.json:27-30</code>), and react additionally sets <code>noFallthroughCasesInSwitch</code> (<code>packages/react/tsconfig.json:29</code>).</li><li><code>@ts-ignore</code>/<code>@ts-expect-error</code> usage is minimal (3 occurrences total, repo-wide, all <code>@ts-expect-error</code> — never the unchecked <code>@ts-ignore</code>) and each is a one-line, explained escape hatch for accessing private/undocumented Ogma internals: <code>packages/core/src/handlers/links/geometry.ts:177</code> (<code>// @ts-expect-error curvature exists on edges</code>), <code>packages/core/src/handlers/links/sync.ts:132</code> (<code>// @ts-expect-error private event</code>), <code>packages/core/src/handlers/snapping/edge.ts:102</code> (<code>// @ts-expect-error curvatures exist is a private readonly attribute</code>).</li><li>Explicit <code>any</code> is essentially absent from application logic; the only hits are three structural callback signatures in a local interface, <code>packages/core/src/ui/panelVisibility.ts:45,47,49</code> (<code>(...args: any[]) =&gt; void</code>), which mirror an external event-emitter shape rather than laziness.</li><li>Types are built via a real declaration pipeline (<code>scripts/build-types.mjs</code>, <code>rollup-plugin-dts</code>) that produces a single shared declaration graph across the <code>index</code>/<code>ui</code> entry points — see the extensive rationale comment in <code>packages/react/tsconfig-build.json:1-24</code> about avoiding duplicated <code>Control</code> declarations between packages.</li><li><code>typescript: 5.3.3</code> pinned identically in both packages&#39; <code>devDependencies</code> (<code>packages/core/package.json</code>, <code>packages/react/package.json</code>).</li></ul><p><strong>Missing / weak:</strong></p><ul><li>No <code>noUncheckedIndexedAccess</code> or <code>exactOptionalPropertyTypes</code> in either tsconfig (<code>grep -rn &quot;noUncheckedIndexedAccess\\|exactOptionalPropertyTypes&quot; packages/*/tsconfig*.json</code> → no hits) — the strict baseline is solid but not maximal; index/record access (e.g. <code>state.features[id]</code>, seen throughout <code>packages/core/src/store/index.ts</code>) is not compiler-checked for possible <code>undefined</code>.</li><li>No <code>.d.ts</code>/type-level tests (no <code>tsd</code>, <code>expect-type</code>, or <code>// $ExpectType</code> usage found) to guard the public API&#39;s exported type surface against accidental narrowing/widening across releases.</li></ul><h2 id="_3-linting-formatting" tabindex="-1">3. Linting/formatting <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-linting-formatting" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3. Linting/formatting&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Exists:</strong></p><ul><li><code>oxlint</code> (a Rust-based ESLint-compatible linter) is used by both packages, extending a shared org config: <code>packages/core/oxlint.json:1-12</code>, <code>packages/react/oxlint.json:1-13</code> both <code>&quot;extends&quot;: [&quot;../../node_modules/@linkurious/eslint-config-ogma/.oxlintrc.json&quot;]</code>. The shared base (<code>node_modules/@linkurious/eslint-config-ogma/.oxlintrc.json</code>, 168 lines) enables a broad set of correctness rules (<code>no-unused-vars</code>, <code>no-fallthrough</code>, <code>no-unsafe-optional-chaining</code>, <code>constructor-super</code>, etc.) and, per-package, <code>react/rules-of-hooks: &quot;error&quot;</code> and <code>react/exhaustive-deps: &quot;warn&quot;</code> (<code>packages/react/oxlint.json:9-10</code>).</li><li>Separate <code>lint</code> (console output) and <code>lint:ci</code> (machine-readable) scripts exist in every package, e.g. <code>packages/core/package.json:33-34</code>: <code>&quot;lint&quot;: &quot;npx oxlint -c oxlint.json src&quot;</code>, <code>&quot;lint:ci&quot;: &quot;mkdir -p reports &amp;&amp; npx oxlint -c oxlint.json -f checkstyle src &gt; reports/checkstyle.xml&quot;</code> (same pattern in <code>packages/react/package.json:24-25</code>), producing a Checkstyle XML report consumable by a CI dashboard.</li><li>Root <code>package.json:26-27</code> fans both out across workspaces: <code>&quot;lint:ci&quot;: &quot;npm run lint:ci -ws&quot;</code>, <code>&quot;lint&quot;: &quot;npm run lint -ws&quot;</code>.</li><li>Prettier is configured (not run separately, inlined per-package): <code>&quot;prettier&quot;: { &quot;printWidth&quot;: 80, &quot;trailingComma&quot;: &quot;none&quot; }</code> in root <code>package.json:52-55</code>, <code>packages/core/package.json</code> (end), and <code>packages/react/package.json</code> (end).</li></ul><p><strong>Missing / weak:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>No accessibility linting.</strong> The shared oxlint config&#39;s <code>plugins</code> array is <code>[&quot;typescript&quot;,&quot;import&quot;,&quot;promise&quot;,&quot;promise&quot;,&quot;unicorn&quot;]</code> (<code>node_modules/@linkurious/eslint-config-ogma/.oxlintrc.json:2-8</code>, note <code>&quot;promise&quot;</code> is listed twice) — no <code>jsx-a11y</code> (or oxlint&#39;s a11y category) plugin is enabled anywhere, and <code>packages/react/oxlint.json</code> doesn&#39;t add one either. Combined with dimension 8 below, accessibility is not checked by tooling at all.</li><li>Whether <code>lint:ci</code> actually gates merges in CI can&#39;t be confirmed from this repo — see dimension 4 (Jenkinsfile doesn&#39;t explicitly declare a lint step; it&#39;s opaque, delegated to the external shared library).</li><li>No standalone Prettier <code>check</code>/<code>format</code> npm script exists in any <code>package.json</code> — formatting is configured but not demonstrably enforced as a separate CI step (it may be folded into oxlint&#39;s own formatting rules, which <code>oxlint.json</code> doesn&#39;t disable, but this isn&#39;t verifiable from config alone).</li></ul><h2 id="_4-ci-cd-pipeline" tabindex="-1">4. CI/CD pipeline <a class="header-anchor" href="#_4-ci-cd-pipeline" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;4. CI/CD pipeline&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Exists:</strong></p><ul><li><code>Jenkinsfile:1-18</code> is the actual CI definition, using a shared <code>linkurious-shared</code> pipeline library (<code>nodeJob</code>): <code>runUnitTests = true</code>, <code>runForwardMerge = true</code>, <code>runNpmPublish = true</code>, <code>createGitTag = true</code> with <code>gitTagPrefix = &#39;v&#39;</code>, <code>runBookeeping = true</code>, and <code>npmPackPaths = [&#39;packages/core&#39;, &#39;packages/react&#39;]</code> (<code>Jenkinsfile:8-17</code>) — i.e. release automation (tagging + npm publish) is wired into CI, not manual.</li><li>Renovate is configured for dependency updates: <code>.github/renovate.json</code> (present, 120 bytes) — confirmed by recent commits like <code>409e09d chore(deps): update dependency ogma-5-3-11 to v5.3.12 (#162)</code> and <code>49128e2 Build(deps-dev): Bump js-yaml from 4.3.1 to 4.3.2 (#157)</code> in <code>git log --oneline</code>.</li><li>Version bump scripts exist at both root and package level: root <code>package.json:19-21</code> (<code>bump:patch/minor/major</code> via <code>npm version -ws --include-workspace-root --no-git-tag-version</code>) and per-package <code>bump2version</code>-based scripts (<code>packages/core/package.json:14-16</code>, <code>packages/react/package.json:15-17</code>), backed by <code>.bumpversion.cfg:1-11</code> (<code>current_version = 2.1.4</code>, semver-shaped <code>parse</code>/<code>serialize</code>). <code>postversion: sync_versions</code> (root <code>package.json:22</code>) and <code>packages/react/package.json:16</code> (<code>&quot;postversion&quot;: &quot;npm i --save @linkurious/ogma-annotations@${npm_new_version}&quot;</code>) keep the two packages&#39; versions and cross-dependency in lockstep.</li><li>Git tags follow <code>vMAJOR.MINOR.PATCH</code> consistently: <code>git tag | sort -V | tail -30</code> shows an unbroken semver sequence from <code>v1.1.1</code> through <code>v2.1.2</code>.</li></ul><p><strong>Missing / weak:</strong></p><ul><li>This analysis cannot confirm from primary sources alone whether <code>lint:ci</code>, <code>test:e2e</code>, or <code>docs:build</code> are actually invoked per-PR by the external <code>linkurious-shared</code> Jenkins library (<code>Jenkinsfile:1</code>) — only <code>runUnitTests</code> is an explicit, visible flag in <code>Jenkinsfile:8-17</code>.</li><li><strong>Git tags lag the published version.</strong> <code>package.json:3</code> and <code>.version</code> both read <code>2.1.4</code>, but the latest git tag is <code>v2.1.3</code> (<code>git tag | sort -V | tail -5</code> → <code>v2.0.3, v2.1.0, v2.1.1, v2.1.2, v2.1.3</code>, no <code>v2.1.4</code> yet). Improved since the first pass of this analysis (tags were two releases behind then, <code>v2.1.2</code>; now one), but still not a reliable real-time record of what&#39;s published to npm.</li><li><strong>No CHANGELOG.</strong> No <code>CHANGELOG.md</code> (or similarly named file) exists anywhere in the repo (<code>find . -iname &quot;changelog*&quot;</code> outside <code>node_modules</code>: no hits). Commit history does follow a loose Conventional Commits style (<code>feat(core): ...</code>, <code>fix(core): ...</code>, <code>test: ...</code>, <code>chore(deps): ...</code> — see <code>git log --oneline -30</code>), which is exactly the input format automated changelog generation (e.g. <code>conventional-changelog</code>, <code>changesets</code>) needs, but nothing in <code>package.json</code> or CI wires that up. Consumers upgrading between versions have no generated record of what changed.</li></ul><h2 id="_5-documentation-completeness" tabindex="-1">5. Documentation completeness <a class="header-anchor" href="#_5-documentation-completeness" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;5. Documentation completeness&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Exists:</strong></p><ul><li>A full VitePress docs site: <code>docs/.vitepress/</code> config (implied by <code>docs:dev</code>/<code>docs:build</code>/<code>docs:preview</code> scripts, root <code>package.json:23-28</code>), with content trees <code>docs/typescript/{api,core-concepts,creating-annotations,managing,styling,ui-components}</code>, <code>docs/react/{core-concepts,creating-annotations,styling,ui-components}</code>, and <code>docs/architecture/</code> (15 top-level files + <code>architecture/react/typescript</code> subtrees per <code>find docs -maxdepth 2 -type d</code>).</li><li>API reference is generated, not hand-written: <code>typedoc</code> + <code>typedoc-plugin-markdown</code> + <code>typedoc-vitepress-theme</code> per package (<code>packages/core/typedoc.json</code>, <code>packages/react/typedoc.json</code>, <code>packages/core/package.json:38</code> <code>&quot;docs:build&quot;: &quot;typedoc &amp;&amp; node scripts/organize-sidebar.mjs&quot;</code>), output into <code>docs/typescript/api</code> and <code>docs/react/api</code> respectively.</li><li>Three machine-readable &quot;AI reference&quot; docs are generated and published: <code>docs/AI_REFERENCE_TYPESCRIPT.md</code> (52.6K), <code>docs/AI_REFERENCE_REACT.md</code> (41.2K), <code>docs/AI_REFERENCE_API.md</code> (63.1K), plus an <code>llms.txt</code> discovery file linked from <code>README.md:16-20</code>. Generation is scripted: <code>scripts/generate-ai-docs.mjs</code>, invoked via <code>postpackage-docs</code> (root <code>package.json:29-30</code>).</li><li><code>docs/getting-started.md</code> (9.6K) is a real onboarding guide with install/usage code blocks (<code>::: code-group</code> npm blocks, TS + React examples) — not a stub.</li><li><code>docs/index.md</code> is a full VitePress &quot;home&quot; landing page with a working quick-example in both TypeScript and React tabs.</li><li>Per-package <code>Readme.md</code> files exist and are non-trivial: <code>packages/core/Readme.md</code> (336B, links into the docs site), <code>packages/react/Readme.md</code> (1.6K, full install + usage example).</li><li>This repo also already has a precedent for ad hoc engineering research notes at <code>docs/notes/sticky-note-author-link-research.md</code> (this file&#39;s sibling), showing an established, if informal, practice of writing fact-finding docs before design work.</li></ul><p><strong>Missing / weak:</strong></p><ul><li><code>docs/examples.md</code> is only 61 bytes (<code>ls docs/*.md</code> output) — essentially a stub/redirect rather than a worked examples page, despite <code>docs/index.md</code> linking to <code>/examples/typescript/basic</code> as a &quot;Need inspiration?&quot; pointer.</li><li>No versioned docs: the VitePress site (<code>docs:publish</code> → <code>gh-pages -t --nojekyll -d dist/docs</code>, root <code>package.json:31</code>) publishes a single latest-only site to GitHub Pages — there&#39;s no per-major-version docs history, so a consumer pinned to an older <code>1.x</code> can&#39;t get matching docs from the published site (only from checking out the corresponding git tag locally).</li></ul><h2 id="_6-error-handling-runtime-robustness" tabindex="-1">6. Error handling &amp; runtime robustness <a class="header-anchor" href="#_6-error-handling-runtime-robustness" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;6. Error handling &amp; runtime robustness&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Exists:</strong></p><ul><li>The store layer validates <em>permission</em> preconditions (editability, cascade-delete safety) before mutating state, always failing soft with a logged error rather than throwing or silently corrupting state — e.g. <code>packages/core/src/store/index.ts:226-270</code> (<code>removeFeature</code>): checks <code>!state.options.isEditable(feature)</code> → <code>console.error(...); return state</code> (unchanged), checks for an orphaned comment before allowing its last arrow to be deleted, and refuses a whole cascade delete if any member is non-editable. The same guard pattern recurs at <code>packages/core/src/store/index.ts:420</code> (update) and <code>packages/core/src/handlers/arrow.ts:330</code> (link creation).</li><li>User-authored text rendered via <code>innerHTML</code> in the Comment renderer is explicitly HTML-escaped first: <code>packages/core/src/renderer/shapes/comment.ts:384-391</code> (<code>formatContent()</code> replaces <code>&amp;</code>, <code>&lt;</code>, <code>&gt;</code> before any further processing), and only then has URLs turned into anchors via a tightly-scoped <code>https?://</code> regex with <code>target=&quot;_blank&quot; rel=&quot;noopener noreferrer&quot;</code> (<code>comment.ts:397-401</code>, pattern from <code>packages/core/src/utils/rendering.ts:7,39-41</code>) — this is a correct, deliberate escape-then-linkify order, not a raw-HTML injection risk.</li><li>Seven <code>try/catch</code> blocks exist at points doing genuinely fallible work (SVG/font/DOM operations): <code>packages/core/src/renderer/shapes/text.ts</code>, <code>packages/core/src/renderer/shapes/index.ts</code>, <code>packages/core/src/handlers/links/geometry.ts</code>, <code>packages/core/src/handlers/links/sync.ts</code>.</li><li><code>packages/core/src/Control.ts:984</code> throws a descriptive <code>Error</code> for a clearly-programmer-error case (<code>getArrow</code> on a non-existent id): <code>if (!arrow) throw new Error(`Arrow with id ${arrowId} not found`);</code>.</li></ul><p><strong>Missing / weak:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>No input validation at the public data-ingestion boundary.</strong><code>packages/core/src/api/update.ts:24-32</code> (<code>UpdateManager.add</code>) accepts any <code>Annotation | AnnotationCollection</code>, runs a format migration (<code>migrateBoxOrTextIfNeeded</code>), and pushes straight into the store with no shape/type check — no verification that <code>annotation.id</code> exists, that <code>annotation.geometry</code> is well-formed GeoJSON, or that <code>annotation.type</code> is one of the known feature types. Repo-wide, <code>grep -rn &quot;throw\\|Error(&quot; packages/core/src/api/*.ts</code> returns <strong>zero matches</strong> — none of <code>comments.ts</code>, <code>drawing.ts</code>, <code>history.ts</code>, <code>selection.ts</code>, <code>update.ts</code> throws or constructs an <code>Error</code> anywhere. Malformed annotation data (e.g. loaded from an external/legacy save) is trusted as-is; failure mode on bad data is presumably a downstream renderer crash rather than a clear validation error at the boundary where it entered.</li><li>No React error boundary exists in <code>packages/react/src</code> (<code>grep -rln &quot;componentDidCatch\\|ErrorBoundary&quot; packages/react/src</code> → no hits) — a render-time exception inside the annotation UI tree (e.g. from the untested <code>ui/</code> components, dimension 1) would propagate up and unmount the host application&#39;s React tree rather than being contained.</li><li><code>console.error</code> is the library&#39;s only &quot;reporting&quot; channel for the guard failures above — there&#39;s no structured error/event (e.g. an emitted <code>&quot;error&quot;</code> event with a code) a host application could subscribe to and handle programmatically instead of scraping the console. Several of these sites carry <code>// eslint-disable-next-line no-console</code> right above them (<code>packages/core/src/store/index.ts:233,245,265</code>, <code>packages/core/src/ui/AnnotationPanel.ts:114</code>), confirming <code>no-console</code> is the linted-for default and these are deliberate, acknowledged exceptions — but still means failures are only observable by reading the browser console, not catchable by the host app.</li><li><strong>Found 2026-09-22, not fixed here (out of scope for the a11y pass that surfaced it):</strong> clicking <em>any</em> button inside the React <code>AnnotationPanel</code> (a color swatch, a background swatch, opening the Font dropdown — even ones that don&#39;t call <code>editor.updateStyle</code>) immediately deselects the annotation and closes the panel, in the unmodified <code>packages/react/web</code> demo app on <code>develop</code> (reproduced via a headless-browser click before any UI changes were made, to rule out a regression from this session&#39;s own edits). <code>AnnotationPanel.tsx:160-173</code>&#39;s <code>stopEvent</code> handler calls <code>evt.stopPropagation()</code> on <code>onClick</code>/<code>onMouseDown</code>/<code>onMouseMove</code> at the panel root specifically to prevent exactly this — so either that propagation-stopping isn&#39;t reaching whatever listener is deselecting (e.g. a capture-phase document listener, which fires before a bubble-phase <code>stopPropagation</code> further down the tree could block it), or the deselection is triggered some other way entirely. Root cause not diagnosed — flagging as a real, reproducible interaction bug for whoever picks it up next; every panel control is currently a one-click-then-gone experience for a mouse user too, not just a keyboard-user a11y gap.</li></ul><h2 id="_7-browser-bundle-concerns" tabindex="-1">7. Browser/bundle concerns <a class="header-anchor" href="#_7-browser-bundle-concerns" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;7. Browser/bundle concerns&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Exists:</strong></p><ul><li>Both packages build with Vite library mode, emitting <strong>ESM (<code>.mjs</code>) and CJS (<code>.js</code>)</strong>, no UMD (explicitly ruled out for react: &quot;Multi-entry libs cannot use UMD; emit ESM (.mjs) and CJS (.js)&quot;, <code>packages/react/vite.config.ts:29-30</code>; core has an <code>&quot;./umd&quot;</code> export subpath in <code>packages/core/package.json:32-36</code> but it actually points at the same <code>.js</code>/<code>.mjs</code> CJS/ESM outputs, not a true global/IIFE UMD bundle).</li><li>Modern package <code>exports</code> maps with <code>types</code>/<code>import</code>/<code>require</code>/<code>default</code> conditions for multiple subpaths (<code>.</code>, <code>./ui</code>, <code>./ui/styles.css</code> or <code>./style.css</code>, <code>./esm</code>) in both <code>packages/core/package.json:12-46</code> and <code>packages/react/package.json:18-33</code>.</li><li>Peer dependencies correctly externalize the host libraries instead of bundling them: <code>@linkurious/ogma</code> is a <code>peerDependency</code> (<code>&gt;=5.3.11</code>) in both packages, <code>react &gt;=17</code> is a peer dep of <code>packages/react</code> (<code>packages/react/package.json:46-49</code>), and both Vite configs list them (plus <code>@linkurious/ogma-react</code>, <code>@linkurious/ogma-annotations</code>(-react), <code>vanilla-colorful</code>) under <code>rollupOptions.external</code> (<code>packages/core/vite.config.ts:25-26</code>, <code>packages/react/vite.config.ts:33-42</code>).</li><li><code>packages/react/package.json:13-15</code> declares <code>&quot;sideEffects&quot;: [&quot;dist/**/*.css&quot;]</code>, correctly scoping tree-shaking side-effect exemptions to just the CSS files.</li><li>A CSS-in-JS injection plugin is used for the React build (<code>vite-plugin-lib-inject-css</code>, <code>packages/react/vite.config.ts:3,17</code>) so consumers don&#39;t have to manually import a separate stylesheet for component styles beyond the dedicated <code>ui/styles.css</code> export.</li><li><code>Control</code> type identity is deliberately kept singular across the <code>index</code>/<code>ui</code> entry points via a real chunked <code>.d.ts</code> build (<code>scripts/build-types.mjs</code>, <code>rollup-plugin-dts</code>) instead of Vite&#39;s default per-entry declaration bundling — extensively documented in <code>packages/core/vite.config.ts:5-13</code> and <code>packages/react/tsconfig-build.json:1-24</code> as a deliberate fix for a previously-real &quot;editor is undefined&quot; dual-context bug (<code>packages/react/vite.config.ts:37-39</code> comment).</li></ul><p><strong>Missing / weak:</strong></p><ul><li><strong><code>packages/core/package.json</code> has no <code>&quot;sideEffects&quot;</code> field at all</strong> (only <code>packages/react/package.json</code> declares one) — despite core also shipping a CSS file (<code>&quot;style&quot;: &quot;dist/ogma-annotations.css&quot;</code>, <code>packages/core/package.json:9</code>). Without an explicit <code>sideEffects</code> marker, bundlers must conservatively assume the whole package has side effects, which weakens tree-shaking for consumers who only import a few functions from core (e.g. just <code>createArrow</code>/<code>createText</code> without the <code>Control</code>/<code>ui</code> machinery).</li><li><strong>Source maps are explicitly disabled</strong> in the core build: <code>packages/core/vite.config.ts:15</code> — <code>sourcemap: false</code>. React&#39;s <code>vite.config.ts</code> doesn&#39;t set the option at all, which is Vite&#39;s <code>false</code> default too (confirmed: <code>grep -n &quot;sourcemap&quot; packages/react/vite.config.ts</code> → no match). Neither published package ships source maps, so debugging a consumer-reported stack trace against a minified build (<code>minify: true</code>, <code>packages/core/vite.config.ts:27</code>) requires the library author to reproduce locally rather than read the trace directly.</li><li>No bundle-size tracking/budget tooling (<code>size-limit</code>, <code>bundlesize</code>, <code>bundlewatch</code> — <code>grep -rn &quot;size-limit\\|bundlesize\\|bundlewatch&quot;</code> across all <code>package.json</code> files: no hits) — bundle size regressions between releases aren&#39;t measured or gated anywhere.</li><li>No true UMD/global (<code>&lt;script&gt;</code>-tag-consumable, IIFE with a global var) build exists for either package despite <code>package-lock.json</code>/<code>exports</code> naming an <code>&quot;./umd&quot;</code> subpath — it&#39;s CJS under a different label, not a browser-global bundle, so a non-bundler, no-build-step consumer (plain <code>&lt;script src=...&gt;</code>) has no supported entry point.</li></ul><h2 id="_8-accessibility" tabindex="-1">8. Accessibility <a class="header-anchor" href="#_8-accessibility" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;8. Accessibility&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong><code>packages/react/src/ui</code> (React UI layer) — closed 2026-09-22.</strong><code>packages/core/src/ui</code> (vanilla imperative toolbar/panel) — still open, see below.</p><ul><li>Every icon-only control across all 16 <code>packages/react/src/ui/**</code> components now carries <code>aria-label</code> (mirroring the existing <code>data-tooltip</code> text — that text was already CSS <code>::after</code>/<code>::before</code> content, which is not exposed to the accessibility tree, so it was previously invisible to screen readers despite being visible to sighted mouse users), plus <code>aria-pressed</code> on toggle-state buttons (<code>AddMenu</code>&#39;s drawing-tool/erase buttons, <code>LineTypeController</code>, <code>BackgroundController</code>, <code>ColorController</code> swatches) and <code>role=&quot;group&quot;</code>/<code>aria-label</code> on the toolbar/swatch-row containers.</li><li><code>FontController</code> and <code>ExtremityController</code>&#39;s &quot;custom-select&quot; dropdowns were plain, unlabeled, keyboard-unreachable <code>&lt;div&gt;</code>s with <code>onClick</code> handlers (no <code>tabIndex</code>, no role, no way to reach or operate them without a mouse). Converted to real <code>&lt;button type=&quot;button&quot;&gt;</code> elements (trigger + each option) with <code>aria-haspopup=&quot;listbox&quot;</code>/<code>aria-expanded</code> on the trigger and <code>role=&quot;listbox&quot;</code>/<code>role=&quot;option&quot;</code>/<code>aria-selected</code> on the option list — native Tab/Enter/Space now reaches and operates every option; the trigger also closes on Escape. <code>packages/core/src/ui/styles.css</code>&#39;s <code>.custom-select-option</code> rule had no <code>background</code>/<code>border</code> reset in its base (non-hover/non-selected) state (only <code>:hover</code>/<code>.selected</code> set <code>background</code>) — converting to <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> would have shown default browser button chrome without also fixing that rule, so it was closed too (<code>background: transparent; border: none;</code> added, verified visually before/after via a headless-browser screenshot comparison against unmodified <code>develop</code>).</li><li><code>SliderController</code>&#39;s <code>&lt;input type=&quot;range&quot;&gt;</code> had no accessible name at all (a sibling <code>&lt;h3&gt;</code> showed the label visually but wasn&#39;t programmatically associated) — now carries <code>aria-label</code> from the same <code>title</code> prop already rendered.</li><li><code>Icon.tsx</code>&#39;s SVGs are now <code>aria-hidden=&quot;true&quot;</code> (decorative — the parent control supplies the accessible name), and <code>AnnotationPanel</code>&#39;s root is <code>role=&quot;region&quot;</code>/<code>aria-label=&quot;Annotation style panel&quot;</code> for landmark navigation.</li><li>Still open in <code>packages/react</code>: no roving-tabindex/arrow-key navigation within an open listbox (Tab moves through every option one at a time, which is keyboard-operable but not the full ARIA listbox pattern); no live-region announcement of selection/drawing-mode changes; the third-party <code>&lt;rgba-color-picker&gt;</code> custom element&#39;s internal accessibility (inside <code>ColorController</code>&#39;s picker overlay) is out of this package&#39;s control.</li><li><strong><code>packages/core/src/ui</code> (vanilla toolbar/panel) is a separate, still-open gap</strong> — same imperative-DOM-construction pattern as before (<code>packages/core/src/ui/toolbar/cells/dropdown.ts:48</code>, <code>packages/core/src/ui/toolbar/cells/buttonItem.ts:21</code>, <code>packages/core/src/ui/AnnotationToolbar.ts:341</code>), still only the one pre-existing <code>aria-pressed</code> hit (<code>packages/core/src/ui/toolbar/cells/buttonItem.ts:33</code>). Not touched by this pass — the shared <code>styles.css</code> fix above benefits it for free, but the imperative TS itself needs the same <code>aria-label</code>/<code>role</code>/keyboard treatment applied separately, since it&#39;s a different codebase (no JSX) from the React work done here.</li><li>No accessibility linting is configured — no <code>jsx-a11y</code> (or oxlint a11y category) plugin in the shared or per-package oxlint config (dimension 3) — still open, would have caught this gap automatically.</li><li>Still consistent with the product surface: a canvas-drawing tool (arrows, text boxes positioned by mouse drag) is inherently hard to make fully keyboard-accessible for the <em>drawing</em> interactions themselves (no keyboard alternative for placement exists yet) — this pass covered the <em>panel/toolbar chrome</em> around that canvas, not the canvas interaction itself.</li></ul><h2 id="_9-security-posture" tabindex="-1">9. Security posture <a class="header-anchor" href="#_9-security-posture" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;9. Security posture&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Exists:</strong></p><ul><li><code>npm audit --omit=dev</code> (production dependency tree only): <strong>0 vulnerabilities.</strong></li><li>Full <code>npm audit</code> (including devDependencies): 8 vulnerabilities (5 low, 2 moderate, 1 high), all in the <strong>dev/build toolchain</strong>, not shipped code: a <code>@tootallnate/once</code> → <code>http-proxy-agent</code> → <code>jsdom</code> chain pulled in transitively by <code>svg-text-to-path</code> (a dependency of <code>@linkurious/svg-font-embedder</code>, a devDependency), and an <code>esbuild</code> issue pulled in transitively by <code>vitepress</code>&#39;s bundled <code>vite</code>. Both report &quot;No fix available&quot; upstream. Neither reaches the published npm packages (core/react <code>dist/</code> output), since <code>&quot;files&quot;</code> in both package.json&#39;s restricts what&#39;s published (<code>packages/core/package.json:16-21</code>, <code>packages/react/package.json:15-17</code> — <code>dist</code> only).</li><li>Text content rendered via <code>innerHTML</code> is escaped before insertion (see dimension 6 — <code>formatContent()</code>, <code>packages/core/src/renderer/shapes/comment.ts:384-391</code>) and the auto-linkification regex only matches <code>https?://</code> (not <code>javascript:</code> or other schemes), with <code>rel=&quot;noopener noreferrer&quot;</code> on generated anchors.</li></ul><p><strong>Missing / weak:</strong></p><ul><li>No <code>SECURITY.md</code> anywhere in the repo (<code>find . -iname &quot;SECURITY.md&quot;</code> outside <code>node_modules</code>: no hits) — no documented vulnerability-disclosure process or contact for a security researcher who finds an issue in a publicly-published npm package.</li><li><code>npm audit</code> is not run in CI as far as this repo shows — <code>Jenkinsfile</code> doesn&#39;t reference <code>audit</code> anywhere, and whether the opaque external Jenkins shared library covers it can&#39;t be confirmed from primary sources (dimension 4) — dependency vulnerabilities are only caught if someone runs <code>npm audit</code> manually or Renovate happens to open an update PR.</li><li>Other <code>innerHTML</code> call sites in <code>packages/core/src/ui/</code> and <code>packages/core/src/renderer/shapes/</code> (<code>dropdown.ts:48</code>, <code>buttonItem.ts:21</code>, <code>AnnotationToolbar.ts:341</code>, <code>arrow.ts:117</code>, <code>index.ts:92,186</code>, <code>comment.ts:286,366</code>) all assign either <code>&quot;&quot;</code> (clearing) or the result of <code>svgIcon(...)</code> (an internal, non-user-controlled icon name) — these are not user-data-reachable, so they don&#39;t carry the same XSS surface as <code>comment.ts:335</code>, but this was only confirmed by reading each call site individually; there&#39;s no lint rule or convention in this codebase flagging <code>innerHTML</code> assignments for manual security review (e.g. no <code>eslint-plugin-security</code>or oxlint equivalent enabled).</li></ul><h2 id="_10-backward-compatibility-semver-discipline" tabindex="-1">10. Backward compatibility / semver discipline <a class="header-anchor" href="#_10-backward-compatibility-semver-discipline" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;10. Backward compatibility / semver discipline&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Exists:</strong></p><ul><li>Version is kept in lockstep across root, <code>packages/core</code>, and <code>packages/react</code> — all read <code>2.1.4</code> (<code>package.json:3</code>, <code>packages/core/package.json:2</code>, <code>packages/react/package.json:2</code>), enforced by the <code>sync_versions</code> postversion hook (root <code>package.json:22</code>) and <code>packages/react/package.json:16</code>&#39;s <code>postversion</code> re-pinning its <code>@linkurious/ogma-annotations</code> dependency to the exact new version.</li><li>Git tags are a clean, monotonic semver sequence (<code>v1.1.1</code> → <code>v2.1.3</code>, <code>git tag | sort -V</code>), and a real major bump exists in history (<code>v1.1.27</code> → <code>v2.0.0</code>, commits <code>2df13ac</code>/<code>6bc2ed3</code>), evidence the project has exercised the major-version-for-breaking-change discipline at least once (that release included a package rename — see commit <code>1c5a4e3 Fix npm publish path in Jenkinsfile after renaming of package.</code>).</li><li>An explicit backward-compat shim exists in code: <code>packages/core/src/api/update.ts:29</code> (<code>migrateBoxOrTextIfNeeded(annotation)</code>) runs on every <code>add()</code> call to migrate an old Polygon-based Box/Text geometry format to the current Point-based format — a real, live example of additive/non-breaking data evolution.</li><li>The peer dependency floor is actively tested, not just declared: <code>scripts/test-ogma-floor.mjs</code> + root <code>package.json:10-11</code> (<code>test:ogma-floor</code>, <code>e2e:ogma-floor</code>) run the suite against the minimum declared Ogma version (<code>ogma-5-3-11</code> devDependency aliasing <code>@linkurious/ogma@5.3.12</code>, root <code>package.json:66</code>) to catch accidental use of newer Ogma APIs than the declared <code>&gt;=5.3.11</code> peer range allows.</li></ul><p><strong>Missing / weak:</strong></p><ul><li>No CHANGELOG (dimension 4) means there&#39;s no per-version record of what was additive vs. breaking — a consumer has to read raw commit history or diff <code>dist/types</code> between tags to know.</li><li>No deprecation pattern found in code: <code>grep</code> for <code>@deprecated</code> JSDoc tags was not run as an exhaustive scan here, but no deprecated-API affordance (e.g. a runtime deprecation warning helper) exists in <code>packages/core/src/api/*.ts</code> or <code>Control.ts</code> — old API surface appears to be removed/renamed directly rather than soft-deprecated first (consistent with the <code>2.0.0</code> rename event above having no visible transition period in this repo).</li><li>Git tags currently lag <code>package.json</code>&#39;s version by one patch release (dimension 4) — semver-as-documented-in-tags is presently unreliable.</li></ul><h2 id="prioritized-punch-list" tabindex="-1">Prioritized punch list <a class="header-anchor" href="#prioritized-punch-list" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Prioritized punch list&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>P0 — blocks &quot;industrial grade&quot; claim outright:</strong></p><ol><li><s><strong>No coverage tooling installed</strong></s> — <strong>closed 2026-09-22.</strong><code>@vitest/coverage-v8</code> installed in both packages, <code>coverage.enabled: true</code> wired into both vitest configs (unconditional, since CI&#39;s <code>test:unit</code> never passes <code>--coverage</code>), <code>test:coverage</code> scripts added. Baseline as of the fix: core 60.14% statements, react 83.88% statements — no threshold enforced yet, so regression isn&#39;t gated, just visible.</li><li><s><strong><code>packages/react/src/ui/</code> (16 components) is untested</strong></s> — <strong>closed 2026-09-22</strong>, see dimension 1 above. 68 new component tests added; the remaining risk here is that nothing yet <em>gates</em> on coverage (item 1 above makes it visible, not enforced), and <code>attachPanelVisibility</code>&#39;s own state machine in <code>packages/core</code> is still untested at the source.</li><li><strong>No input validation at the public API boundary</strong> (dimension 6): <code>UpdateManager.add()</code> (<code>packages/core/src/api/update.ts:24-32</code>) and the rest of <code>packages/core/src/api/*.ts</code> never throw or validate — malformed external data (e.g. hand-edited or legacy saved annotations) is trusted as-is into the store with no defined failure mode.</li></ol><p><strong>P1 — significant gaps, not fully blocking:</strong> 4. <strong>No CHANGELOG</strong> (dimensions 4, 10) despite Conventional-Commits-style history that could drive one automatically. 5. <strong>Git tags lag <code>package.json</code> version</strong> (<code>v2.1.3</code> tag vs. <code>2.1.4</code> in <code>package.json</code>/<code>.version</code>) — the release-tagging automation (<code>Jenkinsfile:13</code>, <code>createGitTag = true</code>) appears to have missed the latest patch release; the tag history isn&#39;t currently trustworthy. 6. <s><strong>No accessibility support or linting</strong></s> — <strong>partially closed 2026-09-22</strong> for <code>packages/react/src/ui</code> (dimension 8): every icon-only control now has <code>aria-label</code>/<code>aria-pressed</code>, the two custom dropdowns are real keyboard-operable buttons with listbox semantics instead of unreachable divs. Still open: <code>packages/core/src/ui</code>&#39;s imperative toolbar/panel (a separate, untouched codebase), and no <code>jsx-a11y</code>-equivalent lint rule enabled anywhere to catch regressions. 7. <strong><code>packages/core/package.json</code> missing <code>&quot;sideEffects&quot;</code> field</strong> (dimension 7) despite shipping CSS — weakens tree-shaking relative to <code>packages/react</code>, which does declare it correctly. 8. <strong>Source maps disabled</strong> in the core build (<code>sourcemap: false</code>, <code>packages/core/vite.config.ts:15</code>) and not enabled in react either — complicates debugging consumer-reported stack traces against minified output. 9. <strong>Found 2026-09-22: clicking any control inside the React <code>AnnotationPanel</code> deselects the annotation and closes the panel</strong> (dimension 6) — reproduced on unmodified <code>develop</code>, not a regression from the a11y work that surfaced it. <code>AnnotationPanel.tsx</code>&#39;s <code>stopPropagation()</code>-based guard against exactly this isn&#39;t preventing it; root cause not yet diagnosed. Every panel control is currently a one-click-then-gone experience, for mouse users too — this is a functional bug, not only an a11y gap.</p><p><strong>P2 — worth doing, lower urgency:</strong> 10. <code>docs/examples.md</code> is a 61-byte stub despite being linked from <code>docs/index.md</code> as the &quot;Need inspiration?&quot; entry point. 11. No <code>noUncheckedIndexedAccess</code>/<code>exactOptionalPropertyTypes</code> — strict baseline is good but not maximal, and the codebase does substantial dictionary-style indexing (<code>state.features[id]</code>). 12. No bundle-size tracking/budget tooling (<code>size-limit</code> or equivalent). 13. No React error boundary in <code>packages/react/src</code> to contain a render failure inside the annotation UI tree. 14. No true browser-global/UMD build for non-bundler <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> consumers, despite an <code>&quot;./umd&quot;</code> export subpath that is actually CJS. 15. <code>npm audit</code> isn&#39;t run in CI (only Renovate PRs surface dependency issues); the 8 existing (all dev-toolchain, no-fix-available) vulnerabilities aren&#39;t tracked/suppressed anywhere. No <code>SECURITY.md</code> exists either (dimension 9) to document a disclosure process.</p>',56)])])}const h=o(s,[["render",d]]);export{g as __pageData,h as default};
